<section id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Implementation details</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the perturbation scale <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a cosine scheduling strategy uniformly across all experiments.
For the maximum perturbation scale, denoted as <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, is adjusted depending on the target dataset. Specifically, we set <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> for the CIFAR-10 dataset, whereas for ImageNet <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>, we utilize <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.
To estimate the divergence, we employed Hutchinson’s trace estimator, approximating the trace with a single noise vector.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Compute-matched baseline</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To establish the wall-clock compute-matched baselines (denoted by <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math> in Table <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Main Experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Refinement of Flow Matching with Divergence-based Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we measured the generation throughput under a single NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU.
To ensure accurate and robust measurements, we recorded the average execution time across 64 independent iterations of the full image generation process, utilizing a fixed batch size of 32.
The detailed computational costs associated with each configuration are summarized in Table <a href="#Pt0.A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 0.C.2 Main Experiments ‣ Appendix 0.C Implementation Details ‣ Training-Free Refinement of Flow Matching with Divergence-based Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.3 </span>Text-to-Image Generation</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the text-to-image generation experiments (Tab. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Text-to-Image Generation ‣ 5.3 FDS on Various Tasks ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Refinement of Flow Matching with Divergence-based Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we utilized the default ODE solver provided in SD3-Medium <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, specifically the Euler solver.
The sampling process was conducted using 50 denoising steps.
Consistent with our ImageNet <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> configuration, we applied a cosine schedule for the noise parameter, setting the maximum perturbation scale to <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.4 </span>Inverse Problem</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the inverse problem experiments, the results of which are presented in Table <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Inverse Problem ‣ 5.3 FDS on Various Tasks ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Training-Free Refinement of Flow Matching with Divergence-based Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we evaluated our method on Gaussian deblurring and super-resolution <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\times 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\times 4)</annotation></semantics></math> tasks.
We adhered to the experimental protocol established by TFG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and adopted the hyperparameter configurations from their official implementation.
Specifically, the sampling process was conducted using 100 denoising steps, with 4 iterations performed per denoising step.
To evaluate the performance across both tasks, we utilized 256 samples from the Cat dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Furthermore, the maximum perturbation scale was task-specifically set to <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for Gaussian deblurring and <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> for super-resolution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.5 </span>HRF Capacity-matched Configuration</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide further details regarding the HRF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> configurations presented in Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1 Comparison with Training-Based Frameworks ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ Training-Free Refinement of Flow Matching with Divergence-based Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we outline the training and architectural specifications for the baseline model.
The HRF model was trained for a total of 400,000 steps, targeting unconditional CIFAR-10 generation.
To ensure a fair comparison by matching the total parameter count (56.0M) with our backbone model, EDM (55.7M), we implemented specific architectural modifications.
Specifically, we augmented the standard architecture by integrating two additional residual blocks into the U-Net and incorporating five extra linear layers into the time conditioning module.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Pt0.A3.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="Pt0.A3.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="Pt0.A3.T5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Quantitative comparison on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet <math id="Pt0.A3.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="Pt0.A3.T5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Results are evaluated using 100 denoising steps.</span></span></figcaption>
